<p class="lede">This site explains the State Pension, the yearly increase known as the triple lock, what a pension can cover, and the evidence for and against changing the rules. It is written for people who have heard these things in the news and want them explained.</p>

<h2 id="three-things">Three things to know first</h2>

<p>The <strong>State Pension</strong> is a regular payment from the government to people who have reached State Pension age and have paid, or been credited with, enough National Insurance. There are two systems. Which one applies depends on when a person was born.</p>

<p>The <strong>triple lock</strong> is the rule for the yearly increase. Each April the full rates rise by the highest of three figures: the rise in prices, the rise in average earnings, or 2.5%. {{ m.c("tl-definition") }}</p>

<p>On {{ ("pm-care-2026-09-29" | sourceRecord).published_date | timeTag | safe }} the government announced that it will change the rule from April 2030. It says it will legislate for the change, and that the triple lock stays until then. {{ m.c("rule-2030") }} <a href="/reform-options/#opt-2030">What the announced rule does</a>.</p>

<p><strong>Entitlement and increases are different questions.</strong> Entitlement decides who gets a State Pension and how much of the full rate. The yearly increase decides how the rates change over time. A proposal to change one does not change the other.</p>

